// Labyrinth - a dungeon loop on the SFC ensemble, after the first two Megami
// Tensei games.
//
// The Super Famicom dungeons are small machines of dread: a steady arpeggio
// that never resolves, a bass in long notes, a melody that climbs and falls
// back, and very little drum. This is written the way those chips were:
// every part is one voice, and nothing is thicker than the channels allow.
//
// Harmony. D minor, with the flat-six and the Neapolitan pulling down onto
// the dominant: Dm Dm Bb A | Dm Gm Eb A. The Eb is the flat second of D, the
// darkest chord in the key, and the melody leans on its Eb the same way.
// The A major at the end of each phrase is the question the loop keeps
// asking.
//
// The arpeggio is the thin 12.5% pulse in eighths, up and back down over
// each chord; the bass is the stepped triangle. The melody is the 25%
// pulse. In b the FM brass takes the chords under it in whole notes, the
// melody moves to the hollow 50% pulse and climbs an octave in its second
// phrase, the bass becomes the FM slap, and the kit comes in - the second
// pass has to feel deeper into the maze. The bell marks the phrase starts.

title: Labyrinth
ensemble: sfc
parts: lead hollow:lead@lead keys arp:keys@keys bell:bells@keys bass slap:bass@bass perc
tempo: 116
meter: 4/4
key: d minor
order: [a b]

